1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:03,334
A woman spills McDonald's coffee on herself

2
00:00:03,334 --> 00:00:05,950
and sues. A jury gives her nearly three

3
00:00:05,950 --> 00:00:08,566
million dollars. You've heard this one.

4
00:00:08,566 --> 00:00:11,181
It's been a punchline for thirty years.

5
00:00:12,450 --> 00:00:14,998
I'm Eric Hoffman. I'm a lawyer, and almost

6
00:00:14,998 --> 00:00:17,424
everything you think you know about this

7
00:00:17,424 --> 00:00:20,033
case is wrong. Not because of spin. Because

8
00:00:20,033 --> 00:00:22,521
of the trial record. Let's open the file.

9
00:00:28,492 --> 00:00:31,764
Here's the version most of us got. Careless

10
00:00:31,764 --> 00:00:34,732
customer. Of course coffee is hot, it's

11
00:00:34,732 --> 00:00:37,548
coffee. Greedy lawsuit, runaway jury,

12
00:00:37,548 --> 00:00:38,690
lottery ticket.

13
00:00:40,158 --> 00:00:42,513
It showed up on late-night TV, in a

14
00:00:42,513 --> 00:00:44,801
Seinfeld episode, and for years in

15
00:00:44,801 --> 00:00:47,628
arguments about lawsuit abuse. What almost

16
00:00:47,628 --> 00:00:50,387
never came with it was the facts. So here

17
00:00:50,387 --> 00:00:50,993
they are.

18
00:00:51,950 --> 00:00:56,309
February 27, 1992. Albuquerque, New Mexico.

19
00:00:56,309 --> 00:00:59,757
Stella Liebeck is 79. She's in the

20
00:00:59,757 --> 00:01:03,711
passenger seat of her grandson's car, a

21
00:01:03,711 --> 00:01:06,956
1989 Ford Probe. No cup holders.

22
00:01:08,200 --> 00:01:10,130
They've just bought breakfast at a

23
00:01:10,130 --> 00:01:12,458
drive-through, and he's parked so she can

24
00:01:12,458 --> 00:01:14,559
add cream and sugar. She puts the cup

25
00:01:14,559 --> 00:01:16,944
between her knees and pulls the lid toward

26
00:01:16,944 --> 00:01:19,045
her. The whole cup goes into her lap.

27
00:01:21,700 --> 00:01:24,082
She was wearing cotton sweatpants. They

28
00:01:24,082 --> 00:01:26,527
soaked up the coffee and held it against

29
00:01:26,527 --> 00:01:27,077
her skin.

30
00:01:28,533 --> 00:01:30,760
This is where the joke falls apart.

31
00:01:30,760 --> 00:01:33,306
Third-degree burns on six percent of her

32
00:01:33,306 --> 00:01:35,534
skin, and lesser burns over sixteen

33
00:01:35,534 --> 00:01:38,271
percent. Third degree means full thickness:

34
00:01:38,271 --> 00:01:40,436
the burn goes through every layer.

35
00:01:42,075 --> 00:01:44,744
Eight days in the hospital. Skin grafts.

36
00:01:44,744 --> 00:01:47,214
She lost about twenty pounds, down to

37
00:01:47,214 --> 00:01:49,684
eighty-three. Two years of treatment.

38
00:01:51,117 --> 00:01:54,448
Why so bad? Temperature. McDonald's own

39
00:01:54,448 --> 00:01:57,951
standard was to hold its coffee at 180 to

40
00:01:57,951 --> 00:02:01,369
190 degrees. An expert testified that at

41
00:02:01,369 --> 00:02:04,872
190, liquid can cause a third-degree burn

42
00:02:04,872 --> 00:02:08,033
in about three seconds. At 180, about

43
00:02:08,033 --> 00:02:09,571
twelve to fifteen.

44
00:02:11,200 --> 00:02:13,338
Coffee tested elsewhere in town was at

45
00:02:13,338 --> 00:02:14,914
least twenty degrees cooler.

46
00:02:15,867 --> 00:02:18,273
Here's the part nobody tells you. She

47
00:02:18,273 --> 00:02:21,070
didn't start by suing. She asked McDonald's

48
00:02:21,070 --> 00:02:23,867
to cover her costs: her medical bills, some

49
00:02:23,867 --> 00:02:26,469
future care, and the income her daughter

50
00:02:26,469 --> 00:02:29,201
lost caring for her. About twenty thousand

51
00:02:29,201 --> 00:02:29,721
dollars.

52
00:02:30,783 --> 00:02:32,674
McDonald's offered eight hundred.

53
00:02:42,533 --> 00:02:45,237
That's when she hired a lawyer. He offered

54
00:02:45,237 --> 00:02:47,748
to settle for three hundred thousand. A

55
00:02:47,748 --> 00:02:50,452
mediator suggested two hundred twenty-five

56
00:02:50,452 --> 00:02:53,221
thousand. McDonald's said no. So it went to

57
00:02:53,221 --> 00:02:53,672
a jury.

58
00:02:54,617 --> 00:02:57,771
August 1994. The trial runs about a week

59
00:02:57,771 --> 00:03:01,005
and a half, and the jury learns something

60
00:03:01,005 --> 00:03:04,081
that changes the case. Between 1982 and

61
00:03:04,081 --> 00:03:07,315
1992, McDonald's received more than seven

62
00:03:07,315 --> 00:03:10,390
hundred reports of people burned by its

63
00:03:10,390 --> 00:03:10,943
coffee.

64
00:03:12,408 --> 00:03:14,971
The company had already paid more than half

65
00:03:14,971 --> 00:03:17,296
a million dollars settling burn claims.

66
00:03:18,742 --> 00:03:21,357
Its quality-assurance manager testified. In

67
00:03:21,357 --> 00:03:23,669
substance, his position was that seven

68
00:03:23,669 --> 00:03:26,224
hundred reports weren't enough to make the

69
00:03:26,224 --> 00:03:28,414
company change how it served coffee.

70
00:03:29,867 --> 00:03:32,487
Put yourself in the jury box. The question

71
00:03:32,487 --> 00:03:34,983
isn't whether coffee should be hot. It's

72
00:03:34,983 --> 00:03:37,479
whether this company knew its coffee was

73
00:03:37,479 --> 00:03:40,162
hot enough to do this, kept serving it that

74
00:03:40,162 --> 00:03:42,596
way, and decided that was an acceptable

75
00:03:42,596 --> 00:03:42,908
cost.

76
00:03:43,867 --> 00:03:46,580
The jury said yes. But they didn't find her

77
00:03:46,580 --> 00:03:49,168
blameless. They put twenty percent of the

78
00:03:49,168 --> 00:03:51,819
fault on her. So her compensatory damages,

79
00:03:51,819 --> 00:03:54,343
two hundred thousand, were cut by twenty

80
00:03:54,343 --> 00:03:56,804
percent, to one hundred sixty thousand.

81
00:04:05,450 --> 00:04:08,624
Then punitive damages. Her lawyer suggested

82
00:04:08,624 --> 00:04:11,652
a number tied to the company's own sales:

83
00:04:11,652 --> 00:04:14,458
two days of McDonald's coffee revenue,

84
00:04:14,458 --> 00:04:17,559
about 1.35 million dollars a day. The jury

85
00:04:17,559 --> 00:04:19,036
awarded 2.7 million.

86
00:04:21,075 --> 00:04:23,128
That's the number that made the news.

87
00:04:23,128 --> 00:04:25,292
Here's what happened next, which almost

88
00:04:25,292 --> 00:04:25,847
never did.

89
00:04:26,783 --> 00:04:29,586
The judge cut the punitive damages to four

90
00:04:29,586 --> 00:04:32,256
hundred eighty thousand, three times the

91
00:04:32,256 --> 00:04:34,792
compensatory award. Total: six hundred

92
00:04:34,792 --> 00:04:36,328
forty thousand dollars.

93
00:04:38,158 --> 00:04:40,764
Both sides were headed for appeal. Instead

94
00:04:40,764 --> 00:04:42,999
they settled, confidentially. Nobody

95
00:04:42,999 --> 00:04:45,605
outside knows the final number, and anyone

96
00:04:45,605 --> 00:04:47,716
who tells you they do is guessing.

97
00:04:49,158 --> 00:04:51,586
Stella Liebeck died in 2004. She was

98
00:04:51,586 --> 00:04:53,812
ninety-one. Her daughter said the

99
00:04:53,812 --> 00:04:56,443
settlement paid for a live-in nurse. By

100
00:04:56,443 --> 00:04:59,007
then, the story had a life of its own.

101
00:05:08,075 --> 00:05:10,955
So what does this case actually stand for?

102
00:05:10,955 --> 00:05:13,629
Not 'you can get rich spilling coffee.'

103
00:05:13,629 --> 00:05:14,520
Three things.

104
00:05:15,783 --> 00:05:18,606
One: comparative fault. The jury put twenty

105
00:05:18,606 --> 00:05:21,363
percent of the blame on her, and her award

106
00:05:21,363 --> 00:05:24,120
shrank to match. Two: punitive damages are

107
00:05:24,120 --> 00:05:26,747
about what a company knew and did. Seven

108
00:05:26,747 --> 00:05:29,570
hundred prior reports were the case. Three:

109
00:05:29,570 --> 00:05:32,064
the system has brakes. A judge cut the

110
00:05:32,064 --> 00:05:34,888
punitive award by more than eighty percent.

111
00:05:36,741 --> 00:05:39,312
The headline was a spilled cup of coffee.

112
00:05:39,312 --> 00:05:41,757
The record was a 79-year-old woman with

113
00:05:41,757 --> 00:05:44,264
full-thickness burns, a company that had

114
00:05:44,264 --> 00:05:46,521
heard it seven hundred times, and an

115
00:05:46,521 --> 00:05:48,213
eight-hundred-dollar offer.

116
00:05:49,158 --> 00:05:52,285
Next time: a case every cop show quotes and

117
00:05:52,285 --> 00:05:55,049
almost nobody knows. Miranda. I'm Eric

118
00:05:55,049 --> 00:05:58,031
Hoffman, and these are The Hoffman Files.

